\section{The noise model: components, calibration, validation and sensitivity analyses}\label{sec:S3}

\subsection{Components and calibration}\label{sec:S3.1}

Every array saw identical noise sources in one analysis band of << F.g2_band_range_hz >>~Hz (zero-phase Butterworth filter of order << F.g2_filter_order >>; equivalent noise bandwidth << F.g2_enbw_hz >>~Hz). The noise had three components: sensor noise, the cortical background and the room field.

Sensor noise was white: << F.g2_squid_mag_asd >>~fT/√Hz for the Neuromag magnetometers and << F.g2_squid_grad_asd >>~fT/(cm\,√Hz) for the gradiometers, the typical values of the TRIUX datasheet \citep{elekta_triux}, and << F.g2_opm_asd_primary >>~fT/√Hz for the OPMs in the primary analysis, swept over << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_range >>~fT/√Hz. The OPM value is a declared parameter, not a measured device value. It is the middle of the sweep and lies within the range of sensor noise quoted by the clinical OPM studies, although those studies state no band for these figures and the device specifications behind them begin at 1~Hz or above \citep{osborne2018}. The top of the sweep is close to the empty-room floor read from the recording of \citet{jas2026}, a reading of its Fig.~9 that assumes its unstated dB reference is 1~fT²/Hz. We report the comparisons across the sweep.

The cortical background consisted of << F.g2_n_background >> independent dipoles normal to the cortex on a << F.g2_bg_grid_mm >>-mm grid, weighted by cortical area. Like the targets, it omitted the cortex within << F.g2_source_skull_clearance_mm >>~mm of the inner skull (<< F.g2_excluded_vertices_pct_1dp >> of the vertices), which holds the sources nearest to every sensor; Section~\ref{sec:S3.6} adds that cortex back. One scale was fitted so that the median good gradiometer's in-band variance equaled the measured brain noise of the sample recording (main text, Section 2.4). This brain noise is the in-band variance of the pre-stimulus windows of its << F.g2_measured_baseline_windows >> stimuli minus that of the empty-room recording, the channel marked bad excluded. The windows begin soon after the previous stimulus, so they hold task-related rather than only resting activity. Spatially correlated variants used correlation lengths of << F.g2_bg_corr_lengths_mm >>~mm. The room field was an external field of << F.g2_room_terms >> terms (uniform plus linear gradient), fitted to the empty-room recording and passed through each array's coil integration.

Each array's oracle covariance was computed in closed form, without sampling:
\begin{equation}
C = \Sigma_{s} + s^{2} L_{\mathrm{bg}} A L_{\mathrm{bg}}^{\mathsf{T}} + E\,\Sigma_{e} E^{\mathsf{T}},
\label{eq:S-covariance}
\end{equation}
where $\Sigma_{s}$ is diagonal, each entry being a channel's squared white-noise spectral density times the filter's equivalent noise bandwidth (<< F.meth_enbw_hz >>~Hz); $L_{\mathrm{bg}}$ holds the array's lead fields of the << F.meth_bg_n_sources >> background dipoles; $A = \mathrm{diag}(a_i)$, $a_i$ being the area of source $i$'s Voronoi cell on the cortex; $E$ holds the array's responses to the external-field terms; and $\Sigma_{e}$ is their coefficient covariance. The background dipoles are independent and cortex-normal, a greedy Poisson-disk subset of the usable white-surface vertices with << F.meth_bg_grid_mm >>-mm minimum spacing (seed << F.meth_seed_g2 >>). Their Voronoi cells partition the << F.meth_bg_area_cm2 >>~cm² of usable cortex (<< F.meth_bg_mean_area_mm2 >>~mm² on average).

Source $i$'s moment variance is $s^{2} a_i$, with $s^{2}$~=~<< F.meth_bg_var_per_mm2_nam2 >>~nAm² per mm². Its standard deviation is << F.meth_bg_sd_per_sqrt_mm2_nam >>~nAm~×~√($a_i$/mm²), or << F.meth_bg_rms_sd_nam >>~nAm root-mean-square (RMS) over the sources. The scale $s^{2}$ was fitted once, so that the good gradiometers' median modeled variance equals their median measured brain noise (<< F.meth_bg_calibration_grad_ft_cm >>~fT/cm RMS), and was kept per unit area on every head. The matrix $E$ is computed through each array's own coil integration points, for << F.meth_env_terms >> external-field terms (<< F.meth_env_uniform_terms >> uniform and << F.meth_env_gradient_terms >> linear-gradient, about << F.meth_env_r0_mm >>~mm in the head frame); $\Sigma_{e}$ was fitted to the band-filtered empty-room recording. Plug-in covariances are Ledoit--Wolf estimates from << F.meth_plugin10s_samples >> or << F.meth_plugin60s_samples >> independent samples, twice the time-bandwidth product of << F.meth_plugin_seconds >>~s.

The room field is removed by the projection
\begin{equation}
P = I - E\left(E^{\mathsf{T}} W E\right)^{-1} E^{\mathsf{T}} W,
\label{eq:S-projection}
\end{equation}
where $W = \Sigma_{s}^{-1}$. It is applied to topographies and covariance alike ($Ps$, $PCP^{\mathsf{T}}$), by the same rule for every array. For Neuromag it acts on all << F.meth_squid_channels >> channels jointly, so its magnetometer and gradiometer comparisons after projection use subsets of jointly projected data. The retained ranks were << F.meth_rank_squid_proj >> of << F.meth_squid_channels >> for Neuromag (<< F.meth_rank_squid_mag_proj >> of << F.meth_squid_mags >> magnetometers, << F.meth_rank_squid_grad_proj >> of << F.meth_squid_grads >> gradiometers), << F.meth_rank_dense_proj >> of << F.meth_dense_sites >> for the dense array and << F.meth_rank_matched_proj >> of << F.meth_matched_sites >> for the site-matched array. Whitening drops eigenvalues of the correlation-normalized covariance below << F.meth_whiten_tol|replace("10⁻¹⁰", "$10^{-10}$") >> of the largest. Relative to the room-field condition without projection, the projection cost Neuromag << F.g2_proj_cost_squid_pct_2sf >> of its detectability, the dense array << F.g2_proj_cost_dense_pct >> and the site-matched array << F.g2_proj_cost_matched_pct >>.

Each OPM's sensitive axis was the normal of the head surface of the boundary-element model (BEM), placed on the scalp from magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) with normals from its own triangles, averaged within << F.meth_opm_axis_radius_mm >>~mm of the site. It deviated from a plane fitted to the dense MRI scalp by a median << F.meth_opm_axis_dev_median_deg >>° (<< F.meth_opm_axis_dev_pctl >> percentile << F.meth_opm_axis_dev_p95_matched_deg >>° site-matched, << F.meth_opm_axis_dev_p95_dense_deg >>° dense). An alternative construction took the adult's stored outer-skin normals, which lie within << F.meth_opm_axis_radial_deg >>° of the radial direction. With those axes at the same sites, the projected dense ratio was << F.meth_hse_radial_dense_proj_ratio_3dp >> << F.meth_hse_radial_dense_proj_ci_3dp >> instead of << F.meth_hse_final_dense_proj_ratio_3dp >>, and << F.meth_hse_radial_dense_proj_45_70_ratio >> instead of << F.meth_hse_final_dense_proj_45_70_ratio >> at << F.meth_hse_deep_lo_mm >>--<< F.meth_hse_deep_hi_mm >>~mm. Nearly radial axes make room-field patterns resemble those of deep sources, so the projection removes more of their signal. With sensor plus brain noise and no projection, the two constructions gave << F.meth_hse_radial_dense_ib_ratio_3dp >> against << F.meth_hse_final_dense_ib_ratio_3dp >>.

The Neuromag model combines the sample recording's VectorView geometry (<< F.meth_squid_mags >> magnetometers, << F.meth_squid_grads >> planar gradiometers) and the T3 coil definitions of MNE-Python (types << F.meth_coil_grad_type >> and << F.meth_coil_mag_type >>; accurate integration over << F.meth_coil_grad_points >> points per gradiometer and << F.meth_coil_mag_points >> per magnetometer). Its white noise is the manufacturer's typical value for the TRIUX system, << F.meth_squid_mag_asd >>~fT/√Hz and << F.meth_squid_grad_asd >>~fT/(cm\,√Hz) (<< F.meth_squid_mag_rms_ft >>~fT and << F.meth_squid_grad_rms_ft_cm >>~fT/cm in the band), with the << F.meth_dewar_mm >>-mm coil-to-helmet spacing. These three values are the typical values of the TRIUX datasheet \citep{elekta_triux}: they describe a representative Neuromag system, not one installation. Noise from the measured empty-room spectrum (<< F.meth_squid_meas_mag_rms_ft >>~fT, << F.meth_squid_meas_grad_rms_ft_cm >>~fT/cm) is a sensitivity analysis. No signal-space separation is applied.

In the band sensitivity analysis (<< F.g2_band_1_10hz_label >>, << F.g2_band_8_30hz_label >> and << F.g2_band_30_80hz_label >>~Hz), the brain scale and the room field were refitted in each band, with and without a first-order << F.g2_band_opm_response_corner_hz >>-Hz OPM response. The dense ratio with sensor plus brain noise was << F.g2_band_1_10hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> at << F.g2_band_1_10hz_label >>~Hz, << F.g2_band_8_30hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> at << F.g2_band_8_30hz_label >>~Hz and << F.g2_band_30_80hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> at << F.g2_band_30_80hz_label >>~Hz (<< F.g2_band_30_80hz_resp_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> with the OPM response), against << F.g2_band_1_40hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> in the analysis band. The site-matched ratio was << F.g2_band_1_10hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>, << F.g2_band_8_30hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> and << F.g2_band_30_80hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>. Sensor noise was << F.g2_band_1_10hz_intrinsic_share_dense_pct >> of a dense-array channel's variance at << F.g2_band_1_10hz_label >>~Hz and << F.g2_band_30_80hz_intrinsic_share_dense_pct >> at << F.g2_band_30_80hz_label >>~Hz, and << F.g2_band_1_10hz_intrinsic_share_squid_pct >> and << F.g2_band_30_80hz_intrinsic_share_squid_pct >> of a Neuromag channel's. We chose a << F.g2_band_lo_hz >>--<< F.g2_band_hi_hz >>~Hz band because the matched filter's gain rests on the lower frequencies, where the brain noise sits.

In a << F.g2_band_3_70hz_label >>~Hz band, typical of clinical spike review, the dense ratio with sensor plus brain noise was << F.g2_band_3_70hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> (<< F.g2_band_3_70hz_resp_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> with the OPM response) and the site-matched ratio << F.g2_band_3_70hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >>, against << F.g2_band_1_40hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> and << F.g2_band_1_40hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> in the analysis band. Their target-bootstrap intervals, << F.g2_band_3_70hz_dense_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >> and << F.g2_band_3_70hz_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >>, are narrower than the parcel bootstrap of the primary comparison (Section~\ref{sec:S1}). With the room field projected out, the two ratios were << F.g2_band_3_70hz_dense_vs_combined_proj_ratio_3dp >> and << F.g2_band_3_70hz_matched_vs_combined_proj_ratio_3dp >>. In that band, sensor noise was << F.g2_band_3_70hz_intrinsic_share_dense_pct >> of a dense-array channel's variance and << F.g2_band_3_70hz_intrinsic_share_squid_pct >> of a Neuromag channel's.

\subsection{What is fitted, calibrated, checked and predicted}\label{sec:S3.2}

Four kinds of agreement must be kept apart (Fig.~\ref{fig:S2}). For the magnetometers, the room field was fitted to the empty-room recording. With the manufacturer's sensor noise it reproduced their empty-room level (<< F.g2_model_empty_room_mag_ft >> against << F.g2_measured_empty_room_mag_ft >>~fT measured) and explained << F.g2_room_explained_mag_pct >> of their empty-room variance. For the gradiometers the room field explained only << F.g2_room_explained_grad_pct >> of that variance. Their empty-room agreement (<< F.g2_model_empty_room_grad_ftcm >> against << F.g2_measured_empty_room_grad_ftcm >>~fT/cm measured) is therefore an independent check of the model's sensor-noise term, the datasheet's typical value, against this one recording, whose level it slightly exceeds. The gradiometer brain noise is calibrated: << F.g2_model_brain_grad_ftcm >>~fT/cm, equal to the measurement by construction.

The magnetometer brain noise is the one independent prediction of the background at the level of a median, and it fell short: << F.g2_model_brain_mag_ft >>~fT modeled against << F.g2_measured_brain_mag_ft >>~fT measured (<< F.g2_brain_mag_model_over_measured_ratio >> times). The measured value is the excess of the task baseline over the empty room, which also holds non-cortical physiological fields that the model omits by design. The shortfall is thus not purely an error of the cortical background; Section~\ref{sec:S3.4} separates the heart's part. Nothing about the OPM noise was measured: its sensor noise is declared, and its brain and room noise are the Neuromag-calibrated model seen through the OPM arrays' own lead fields.

Two published simulation studies check shape and level. The depth-orientation maps of \citet{hunold2016}, adapted to this anatomy, correlated with the published dipole maps at r~=~<< F.g1b_dipole_r_range >> but reached only << F.g1b_dipole_level_range >> times their level (patch maps: r~=~<< F.g1b_patch_r_range >>, << F.g1b_patch_level_range >> times), even with a background scale fitted to the paper's example traces. The calibration rule of \citet{goldenholz2009} gave a noise-source amplitude within their published range once normalized to their number of sources (<< F.g1c_source_sd_4000_nam >> against << F.g1c_paper_source_sd_nam_range >>~nAm; << F.g1c_source_sd_nam >>~nAm on our grid). Each adaptation keeps its paper's design rather than this study's model. For \citet{hunold2016} these are << F.g1b_dipole_peak_nam >>-nAm spike-wave sources over a background of randomly placed cortical dipoles, conductivities of << F.g1b_conductivity >>~S/m and their peak-to-peak SNR. For \citet{goldenholz2009} they are << F.g1c_focal_nam >>-nAm dipoles, channel-averaged power SNR and a cortical background calibrated on recorded noise.

Both adaptations already show the dependence on metric that the main text examines. With brain noise only, the best single channel of a site-matched OPM array in our adaptation of the dipole maps of \citet{hunold2016} reached only << F.g1b_opm_vs_grad_rows_range >> times the SNR of the planar gradiometers in every depth row. It reached << F.g1b_opm_vs_mag_rows_range >> times that of the magnetometers, falling with depth. With the channel-averaged SNR of \citet{goldenholz2009}, by contrast, the OPM was within about 0.1~dB of both (point estimates: << F.g1c_opm_minus_grad_focal_brain_only_db >>~dB against the gradiometers and << F.g1c_opm_minus_mag_focal_brain_only_db >>~dB against the magnetometers).

In this model brain noise dominates both kinds of magnetometer channel. It was << F.g2_noise_share_dense_brain_pct >> of a dense-array OPM channel's in-band variance (room field << F.g2_noise_share_dense_room_pct >>, sensor noise << F.g2_noise_share_dense_sensor_pct >>) and << F.g2_noise_share_squid_mag_brain_pct >> of a Neuromag magnetometer's (room field << F.g2_noise_share_squid_mag_room_pct >>, sensor noise << F.g2_noise_share_squid_mag_sensor_pct >>), whereas sensor noise was << F.g2_noise_share_squid_grad_sensor_pct >> of a gradiometer's (Fig.~\ref{fig:S2}A and C). In RMS terms, a dense-array channel carried << F.g2_noise_dense_brain >>~fT of brain noise, << F.g2_noise_dense_room >>~fT of room field and << F.g2_noise_dense_intrinsic >>~fT of sensor noise in the band, against << F.g2_noise_squid_mag_brain >>, << F.g2_noise_squid_mag_room >> and << F.g2_noise_squid_mag_intrinsic >>~fT at the magnetometers.

The implied single-channel noise ratio OPM/magnetometer, the analogue of $\eta$, was about << F.g2_noise_rms_dense_vs_mag_noroom_ratio_1dp >> in the << F.g2_band_range_hz >>~Hz band with sensor plus brain noise, the primary condition (<< F.g2_noise_rms_dense_vs_mag_ratio_1dp >> with the room field added). In the recording of \citet{jas2026} it is about << F.lit_sef_noise_ratio_closest >>, over a much wider band (<< F.lit_sef_band_range_hz >>~Hz). Approximate readings of the preprint's spectra, not results of this study, locate the difference. Where brain activity dominates, the OPM/SQUID ratio of the spectral peaks is about << F.lit_sef_midband_noise_ratio >>, somewhat below the model's << F.g2_noise_rms_dense_vs_mag_noroom_ratio_1dp >>. The measured OPM, however, has an empty-room floor of about << F.lit_sef_opm_floor_asd_range >>~fT/√Hz and a distance-independent low-frequency component of about << F.lit_sef_opm_lowfreq_peak_asd_range >>~fT/√Hz at its peak. On this reading, the model lacks the OPM's own floor and a non-brain low-frequency component, which would explain why the measured OPM noise barely fell when the sensors were lifted. The ratio of the spectral peaks and the floor are uncertain readings of the preprint's Fig.~9, and the preprint (version 1) has not been peer reviewed.

