\subsection{Why the ratio falls with depth, and the other adult conditions}\label{sec:S3.3}

Against Neuromag's << F.g2_squid_channels >> channels, the fall of the dense ratio with depth was already present with sensor noise alone, from << F.g2_depth_dense_vs_combined_int_10_15_ratio >> to << F.g2_depth_dense_vs_combined_int_bins_min >>. Neuromag's detectability with sensor noise only rests on its magnetometers, and the peak-field ratio OPM/magnetometer fell from << F.g2_leadfield_dense_vs_mag_10_15_ratio >> in the shallowest bin to << F.g2_leadfield_dense_vs_mag_bins_min >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_55_60_mm >>~mm. The detectability ratio fell with it, though less steeply, since the whole-array measure draws on the field at many channels.

Against the << F.g2_squid_grad_channels >> gradiometers alone, the ratio with sensor noise only barely changed with depth (<< F.g2_depth_dense_vs_grad_int_10_15_ratio >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_10_15_mm >>~mm, << F.g2_depth_dense_vs_grad_int_20_25_ratio >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_20_25_mm >>~mm and << F.g2_depth_dense_vs_grad_int_60_65_ratio >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_60_65_mm >>~mm). With brain noise, however, it fell from << F.g2_depth_dense_vs_grad_ib_10_15_ratio >> to << F.g2_depth_dense_vs_grad_ib_35_40_ratio >> at << F.g2_depth_bin_35_40_mm >>~mm. Here the depth dependence comes from brain noise, which costs every array most for deep sources.

Against all << F.g2_squid_channels >> channels, adding brain noise multiplied the dense array's ratio by a nearly constant factor in every depth bin (<< F.g2_brainmult_dense_vs_combined_range >>). For the site-matched array the factor rose with depth (<< F.g2_brainmult_matched_vs_combined_range >>) and partly offset the geometric fall. The size of the factor follows the per-channel noise budget rather than better whitening by the denser array. Brain noise multiplied the in-band noise of a Neuromag magnetometer about << F.g2_noise_inflation_squid_mag_1dp >>-fold over its sensor floor but that of an OPM only about << F.g2_noise_inflation_dense_1dp >>-fold (a ratio of << F.g2_noise_inflation_mag_over_dense_ratio >>), and the sparser site-matched array had the larger factor.

