A covariance estimated from << F.g2_plugin10s_samples >> samples cost Neuromag more than the OPMs (<< F.g2_plugin10s_loss_squid_combined_ib_pct >> of the oracle detectability, against << F.g2_plugin10s_loss_dense_ib_pct >> for the dense array and << F.g2_plugin10s_loss_matched_ib_pct >> for the site-matched array). It raised the dense ratio to << F.g2_plugin10s_dense_vs_combined_ib_ratio >> and the site-matched ratio to << F.g2_plugin10s_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio >>. With << F.g2_plugin60s_samples >> samples, the declared << F.wr_cov_seconds_long >>-s scenario, the penalty was small for every array (main text, Fig.~2). The site-matched array's interval lay above 1 at an OPM white noise of << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_10 >>~fT/√Hz (<< F.g2_sens_asd10ft_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> << F.g2_sens_asd10ft_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >>) and below 1 at << F.g2_opm_asd_sweep_20 >>~fT/√Hz (<< F.g2_sens_asd20ft_matched_vs_combined_ib_ratio_3dp >> << F.g2_sens_asd20ft_matched_vs_combined_ib_ci_3dp >>).

