\subsection{The model against the measured Neuromag covariance}\label{sec:S3.4}

The measured brain noise is the covariance of the sample recording's << F.g2_measured_baseline_windows >> pre-stimulus windows in the << F.rev_cov_band_hz >>~Hz band minus that of the empty-room recording. As in the adult comparison, the bad channel (<< F.rev_cov_bad_channels >>) was left out and no signal-space projection was applied; << F.rev_cov_det_n_channels_combined >> channels remain (<< F.rev_cov_det_n_channels_mag >> magnetometers, << F.rev_cov_det_n_channels_grad >> gradiometers). Two quantities of the model are fitted to this dataset: the one scalar of the cortical background (the median good-gradiometer brain variance) and the room field's << F.meth_env_terms >>~×~<< F.meth_env_terms >> coefficient covariance (the empty room). Everything else was compared as an independent prediction. This covers the magnetometers' brain-noise amplitude and channel pattern, the gradiometer level at held-out sites, the spatial correlations against inter-sensor distance, the eigenvalue spectra and dominant subspaces, the sub-band spectra, and Neuromag's detectability with the measured covariance.

Where a sample covariance is compared with an exact one, the comparison is calibrated on surrogates. An exact model ``measured'' the same way (phase-randomized records recolored to the model, with the same number of samples) gives the range that an exact model would show. A value outside that range is therefore a real departure of the measured noise from the model.

The model predicted the magnetometers' median brain-noise amplitude at << F.rev_cov_brain_mag_amp_ratio >> of the measured; an exact model would give << F.rev_cov_brain_mag_amp_null_range >>. For the gradiometers, the scale fitted on half the sites predicted the other half's median amplitude to << F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ratio >> << F.rev_cov_heldout_random_grad_amp_ci >> over << F.rev_cov_heldout_n_splits >> random halves (Fig.~\ref{fig:S3}B). Fitted on the inferior half, it predicted the superior half to << F.rev_cov_heldout_inf_to_sup_grad_amp_ratio >>, and the other way round to << F.rev_cov_heldout_sup_to_inf_grad_amp_ratio >>: the model's spatial distribution of brain noise is too flat from bottom to top.

